  <!-- =======================================================
       ELEMENT 4 — HOW TO USE THIS TOOL
       ======================================================= -->
  <section class="medc-section" aria-labelledby="how-to-heading">
    <h2 id="how-to-heading">How to Use This Calculator — Step by Step</h2>

    <ol class="medc-steps">
      <li>
        <strong>Step 1 — Enter your birth month and year.</strong> Select the month and year you were born. You can find this on any government-issued ID, your Social Security card, or your Medicare card if you already have one. The calculator uses your birth date to determine when your 7-month IEP (Initial Enrollment Period) opens and closes, and when your Medicare Part A and Part B coverage can begin.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Step 2 — Select your enrollment window.</strong> Choose which enrollment period applies to you: IEP Early (the three months before your birth month), IEP Birth Month, IEP Late (months five, six, or seven of your IEP), GEP (General Enrollment Period — January through March if you missed your IEP), or SEP (Special Enrollment Period — within eight months of losing active employer coverage). If you are not sure which applies, use IEP Birth Month as your starting point.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Step 3 — Check the first-of-month box if applicable.</strong> If your birthday falls on the first day of any month — for example January 1, March 1, or July 1 — check this box. The "born on the first" rule shifts all your enrollment dates one month earlier. This is a statutory rule that shifts your exact coverage start date.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Step 4 — Click "Calculate Effective Start Dates."</strong> The calculator instantly shows your exact Medicare Part A and Part B coverage effective start dates based on the official CMS and SSA rules. Review both dates carefully. Your Part B start date is the one that determines when your doctor visits and outpatient care are covered by Medicare.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <!-- Pro Tip -->
    <div class="medc-pro-tip">
      <p class="medc-pro-tip-label">💡 Pro Tip</p>
      <p>
        Apply for Medicare during the three months <em>before</em> your birth month so your red, white, and blue Medicare card arrives in the mail before your coverage starts. Processing takes 2–4 weeks. Apply online in 10 minutes at <a href="https://www.ssa.gov/medicare" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ssa.gov/medicare</a> — no office visit required. Read our complete guide on <a href="/tools/medicare-enrollment-deadline-calculator/">how to calculate your Medicare enrollment window and deadlines</a> to make sure you never miss your opening window.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- =======================================================
       ELEMENT 5 — REAL-LIFE SCENARIOS
       ======================================================= -->
  <section class="medc-section medc-section--tinted" aria-labelledby="scenarios-heading">
    <h2 id="scenarios-heading">Illustrative Examples — How Coverage Start Dates Work in Practice</h2>
  <p style="font-size: 0.85rem; font-style: italic; color: #4B5A6E; margin: 0 0 12px 0;">This is a hypothetical example, not a real person.</p>
    <p>These three realistic situations show exactly how the enrollment window you choose determines your Medicare start date — and why the timing matters.</p>

    <!-- Scenario 1 -->
    <div class="medc-scenario-card">
      <div class="medc-scenario-header">
        <span class="medc-scenario-tag">Example 1</span>
        <strong class="medc-scenario-name">Barbara, born March 15, 1961 — Retiring in February 2026</strong>
      </div>
      <p class="medc-scenario-desc">Barbara plans to retire and lose her employer health insurance on February 28, 2026. She turns 65 in March 2026. She enrolls in Medicare in January 2026 — three months before her birth month.</p>
      <table class="medc-table">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Input</th><th scope="col">Value</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Birth month/year</td><td>March 1961</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Birthday on the 1st?</td><td>No (March 15)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Enrollment window</td><td>IEP Early — enrolled January 2026</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p class="medc-result">
        <span class="medc-result-highlight">Part A coverage starts: March 1, 2026</span>
        <span class="medc-result-highlight">Part B coverage starts: March 1, 2026</span>
      </p>
      <p class="medc-action">Barbara's coverage begins exactly when her employer plan ends — no gap, no overlap. She had peace of mind that her transition was smooth because she enrolled three months early.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- Scenario 2 -->
    <div class="medc-scenario-card">
      <div class="medc-scenario-header">
        <span class="medc-scenario-tag">Example 2</span>
        <strong class="medc-scenario-name">Robert, born July 1, 1961 — The "Born on the First" Rule</strong>
      </div>
      <p class="medc-scenario-desc">Robert's birthday is July 1. He assumed his IEP started in April (three months before July). In reality, the "born on the first" rule means Medicare treats him as turning 65 in June — making his IEP start in March.</p>
      <table class="medc-table">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Input</th><th scope="col">Value</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Birth month/year</td><td>July 1961 — born on the 1st</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Birthday on the 1st?</td><td>Yes (July 1)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Enrollment window</td><td>IEP Early — enrolled March 2026</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p class="medc-result">
        <span class="medc-result-highlight">Effective month for IEP calculation: June 2026 (not July)</span>
        <span class="medc-result-highlight">Part A and Part B coverage starts: June 1, 2026</span>
      </p>
      <p class="medc-action">Because of the "born on the first" rule, coverage starts June 1 instead of July 1. Employer health coverage should end May 31 to avoid paying duplicate premiums in June.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- Scenario 3 -->
    <div class="medc-scenario-card">
      <div class="medc-scenario-header">
        <span class="medc-scenario-tag">Example 3</span>
        <strong class="medc-scenario-name">Carol, born October 8, 1960 — Enrolled Late During IEP</strong>
      </div>
      <p class="medc-scenario-desc">Carol was born October 8, 1960. She turned 65 in October 2025 and enrolled in January 2026 (month seven of her IEP, the third month after her birth month).</p>
      <table class="medc-table">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Input</th><th scope="col">Value</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Birth date</td><td>October 8, 1960</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Birthday on the 1st?</td><td>No (October 8)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Enrollment timing</td><td>Enrolled January 2026 (month 7 of IEP)</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p class="medc-result">
        <span class="medc-result-highlight">Premium-free Part A starts: Oct 1, 2025</span>
        <span class="medc-result-highlight">Part B starts: Feb 1, 2026</span>
      </p>
      <p class="medc-action">Carol's Part B coverage started Feb 1, 2026 (the first day of the month after enrollment). Premium-free Part A started Oct 1, 2025 (retroactive to the month she turned 65). She had a 4-month Part B gap from October 2025 to January 2026 before outpatient doctor coverage took effect.</p>
    </div>

    <p class="medc-scenario-note">These are representative examples based on common situations. Individual enrollment dates and effective dates depend on your specific birth date, enrollment timing, and type of enrollment period. Always verify your specific dates with the Social Security Administration at <a href="https://www.ssa.gov/medicare" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ssa.gov/medicare</a> before making coverage decisions.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- =======================================================
       ELEMENT 6 — COMMON PITFALLS
       ======================================================= -->
  <section class="medc-section" aria-labelledby="pitfalls-heading">
    <h2 id="pitfalls-heading">Common Medicare Enrollment Timing Mistakes and How to Avoid Them</h2>

    <h3>Mistake 1 — Confusing the IEP End Date with the SEP End Date</h3>
    <p>Many seniors believe they have eight months to enroll in Medicare after turning 65 because they heard about the eight-month Special Enrollment Period. But the SEP only applies when you had active employer group health coverage during the delay. The IEP (Initial Enrollment Period) is only seven months total — three months before your birth month, your birth month, and three months after. If you do not qualify for an SEP and miss the IEP, you must wait for the GEP (General Enrollment Period) which only runs January through March.</p>
    <p><strong>The fix is</strong> to use this calculator to determine which enrollment period applies to you before your 65th birthday and mark the close date on your calendar. Verify which enrollment period covers your situation at <a href="https://www.medicare.gov/basics/get-started-with-medicare/sign-up/when-does-medicare-coverage-start" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">medicare.gov — when does Medicare coverage start</a>.</p>

    <h3>Mistake 2 — Not Knowing About the "Born on the First" Date Shift</h3>
    <p>Individuals born on the first day of any month have an IEP start date and coverage effective date one month earlier than typical enrollees. Seniors born on January 1, April 1, July 1, or October 1 who do not account for this rule risk misalignment between employer coverage termination and Medicare effective dates.</p>
    <p><strong>The fix is</strong> to always check the "born on the first" box in this calculator if your birthday is on the first day of any month, and verify your IEP dates at <a href="https://www.ssa.gov/medicare" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ssa.gov/medicare</a>.</p>

    <h3>Mistake 3 — Assuming COBRA Coverage Extends the IEP Window</h3>
    <p>One of the most expensive Medicare enrollment mistakes is staying on COBRA continuation coverage after leaving a job and assuming the eight-month SEP clock starts when COBRA runs out. It does not. The SEP 8-month clock starts the day your active employment ends — even if you immediately choose COBRA. Seniors who spend 18 months on COBRA and then try to enroll in Medicare typically discover they are way past their penalty-free window and owe a permanent late enrollment surcharge.</p>
    <p><strong>The fix is</strong> to enroll in Medicare Part B within eight months of leaving active employment — regardless of COBRA. See the official rule at <a href="https://www.medicare.gov/basics/costs/medicare-costs/avoid-penalties" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">medicare.gov — avoid Medicare penalties</a>. Use our <a href="/tools/medicare-late-enrollment-penalty-calculator/">Medicare Part B late enrollment penalty calculator</a> to see exactly what a delay will cost you.</p>

    <h3>Mistake 4 — Missing the GEP Deadline and Waiting Another Full Year</h3>
    <p>If you miss your IEP and do not qualify for an SEP, you can only enroll during the GEP (General Enrollment Period) from January 1 through March 31. Miss the March 31 deadline, and you must wait until the next January. That means potentially going without Medicare Part B coverage for up to nine additional months. During that time, every doctor visit, outpatient procedure, and specialist appointment is fully out-of-pocket.</p>
    <p><strong>The fix is</strong> to mark January 1 as your GEP open date and enroll as early in January as possible. Coverage under the updated GEP rules begins on the first of the month following your enrollment. Enroll in January and coverage starts February 1 — wait until March and coverage does not start until April 1. See GEP rules at <a href="https://www.medicare.gov/basics/get-started-with-medicare/sign-up" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">medicare.gov — how to sign up</a>.</p>
  </section>

